 "source_type": "instagram",
 "source_url": "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DdZWWrmDbwQ/",
 "title": "3-Step Framework for Handling 'Working With Someone Else' Objection",
 "topic": "sales",
 "summary": "Amir Syed outlines a three-step framework to respond when a prospect says they are already working with someone else. Instead of shrinking back or attacking the competitor, sales reps should compliment the prospect, get curious, and create contrast. The goal is to separate having an advisor from being fully taken care of while making the buyer feel smarter.",
 "key_points": [
  "Avoid shrinking back or attacking competitors when a client says they already have someone.",
  "Step 1 (Give a compliment): Say, 'Thank you for sharing that. For a decision this important, I respect that you've already sought advice. I do the same.'",
  "Step 2 (Get curious): Ask, 'Can I ask what matters most to you as you compare the advice that you're getting?'",
  "Step 3 (Create contrast): Ask, 'Do you completely feel confident you're getting everything you need, or are you open to getting a second opinion?'",
  "Help the buyer see that having an advisor already and being fully taken care of are two different things.",
  "Great salespeople do not try to make the competition look worse; they make the buyer feel smarter."
 ],
 "content": "Post text: There are two ways salespeople usually respond when a prospect says they already have someone. Neither one works. Some shrink back immediately, apologizing for even asking. Others go on the attack, trying to make the competitor look bad. Both approaches make the buyer trust you less, not more. This is the better move: 1. Respect the decision they already made. 2. Get curious about what actually matters to them in that relationship. 3. Then create a little space between having an advisor and being fully taken care of, because those are not the same thing. This works because it never asks the buyer to admit they made a bad choice. It just gives them a new way to measure whether the choice they made is still serving them. That is a much easier door to walk through than defending a decision they feel attacked over. The salespeople who win these conversations are not the ones who make the competition look worse. They are the ones who make the buyer feel sharper for asking the next question. That is the whole game when you hear the words I already have someone. If you want to improve the way your business sells and closes, comment 'SALE' and my team will reach out. Make a note of this post. Transcript: When someone says I'm already working with someone else, most sales people either curl up like a roly poly or they attack the competition like a little chihuahua. Do not do that. Use this three-step proven framework: Give compliment. Get curious. Create contrast. First, give a compliment. I want you to say: 'Thank you for sharing that. For a decision this important, I respect that you've already sought advice. I do the same.' Two is get curious. 'Can I ask what matters most to you as you compare the advice that you're getting?' Three is create contrast. 'Do you completely feel confident you're getting everything you need, or are you open to getting a second opinion?' Now, notice what happened. I did not criticize the person that they're working with. Losers do that. I didn't ask them to give me a chance. And I didn't try to force my way past their objection. I simply helped them to see having someone already and being fully taken care of are two different things. Great salespeople do not make the competition look worse, they make the buyer feel smarter. Save this for the next time you hear I'm already working with someone else. Let's go. On-screen text: Client says 'I'm working with someone else'",
 "status": "done",
